body:
**Symphony** is a specification — and a reference implementation — for turning a *Linear* project board into an autonomous coding factory. Released by OpenAI in spring 2026, it describes a long-running daemon that wakes every thirty seconds, reads the board for eligible issues, and dispatches each to an isolated *Codex* agent worker. The spec is language-agnostic; the reference implementation, written in Elixir using the *BEAM* concurrency runtime, exploits lightweight processes to hold dozens of workers cheaply in parallel.

@odysseus0z's summary — “a cron job dispatching tickets from Linear to workers, each of which is a Ralph loop” — is architecturally precise. The orchestrator is not literally a cron job but a daemon running a *poll-tick-dispatch* cycle that behaves identically from the outside. Each worker runs as a *Ralph loop*: an agent that keeps executing turns on the same thread, re-reading issue state after each pass, until the Linear issue moves to a terminal state or a configured turn limit is reached. The name, coined by developer Geoffrey Huntley and drawn from Ralph Wiggum of *The Simpsons*, evokes cheerful, unkillable persistence. It contrasts with the *ReAct* pattern, in which an agent decides for itself when to stop; in a Ralph loop, an external verifier makes that call.

The architectural elegance is in what Symphony deliberately omits: a database. All durable state lives in Linear and Git. What passes for a scratchpad — the running notes an agent accumulates across turns — is written into a Linear comment on the issue itself. That comment survives context rotation, is visible to human reviewers, and persists across daemon restarts. The design keeps the system observable without additional instrumentation: reading the board tells you exactly what every worker is doing. @odysseus0z reported loading fifty Linear tickets before bed and waking to thirty merged pull requests, agents having independently discovered how to test an Electron application by attaching to it via the Chrome DevTools Protocol.

OpenAI claims a **500% increase in landed pull requests** after adopting Symphony internally — a figure striking enough to invite scrutiny. The spec's own authors are circumspect, labelling it an “engineering preview for trusted environments only,” and outside analysis has surfaced three structural constraints. Teams reportedly begin writing vaguer tickets once they assume the agent will compensate, producing architecturally wrong code. Agent output volume can outpace the human review bandwidth that maintains quality. And the binding technical requirement is not model capability but what analysts have called *harness engineering*: Symphony works best in modular, well-typed codebases with deterministic test suites; legacy and unstructured systems are poor candidates.

@odysseus0z closes with “Yes it is all you need” — a deliberate echo of the 2017 paper “Attention Is All You Need,” which introduced the transformer architecture by arguing that competing complexity was unnecessary. The parallel is pointed: the same instinct toward minimal, composable design that reshaped deep learning may be the right frame for agent orchestration. A cron job, some workers, and a comment thread may indeed be enough.
